WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.994
A home timeline is the recent posts of everyone you follow.

2
00:00:06.994 --> 00:00:09.177
Cleo follows Ana, Ben and Eli.

3
00:00:09.367 --> 00:00:13.699
The simplest design stores each post once, in its author's own list.

4
00:00:13.699 --> 00:00:15.801
When Ana posts, that's one write.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.718
When Cleo opens the app, the service reads all three lists and merges them: three lookups.

6
00:00:21.718 --> 00:00:25.912
The work happens on every read, so this is called fan-out on read.

7
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:32.694
Say 10 million users are online, each following 200 accounts and refreshing every 5 seconds.

8
00:00:32.694 --> 00:00:36.780
That's 2 million reads a second, and 400 million lookups.

9
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:38.001
Turn it around.

10
00:00:38.001 --> 00:00:43.861
Keep a ready-made timeline for every user, and do the work once, when a post arrives.

11
00:00:43.861 --> 00:00:45.584
This is fan-out on write.

12
00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:50.213
Ana has three followers, so her new post goes into three timelines.

13
00:00:50.213 --> 00:00:51.540
That's three writes.

14
00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:55.815
Cleo's read is now one lookup, of a list that's already merged.

15
00:00:56.033 --> 00:01:05.140
With the same numbers, 5,800 posts a second to 200 followers each is 1.16 million writes a second.

16
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.207
That's about 345 times less work.

17
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:13.399
It wins because people read their timelines far more often than their friends post.

18
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.094
Now the celebrity.

19
00:01:15.094 --> 00:01:19.491
In 2012 one Twitter account had 31 million followers.

20
00:01:19.491 --> 00:01:22.644
One post from it is 31 million writes.

21
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:27.686
Twitter's workers reached a million followers in about 3.5 seconds.

22
00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:31.452
At that rate, this one post takes about 108 seconds.

23
00:01:31.452 --> 00:01:32.539
The goal was 5.

24
00:01:32.733 --> 00:01:37.796
The workers take posts from a queue, so every ordinary post behind it waits too.

25
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.311
The fix is a hybrid.

26
00:01:39.311 --> 00:01:42.521
Accounts above a follower limit skip the fan-out.

27
00:01:42.521 --> 00:01:44.356
Their posts are stored once.

28
00:01:44.533 --> 00:01:49.280
When Cleo reads, the service merges her timeline with the celebrity's list.

29
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:50.989
Two lookups instead of one.

30
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:54.626
Precompute timelines for the many ordinary accounts.

31
00:01:54.626 --> 00:01:57.687
Merge in the few huge ones when someone reads.
